    <!-- ══ BENEFIT ROWS ══ -->
    <section class="of-benefits">
      <div class="of-benefits__container">
        <div class="of-benefits__header"><div class="of-benefits__eyebrow">Facility Performance</div><h2 class="of-benefits__headline">Security, Energy, Preservation, and Privacy in One Scope</h2><p class="of-benefits__intro">Government window film for Philadelphia supports the work facility teams already manage: safety review, energy planning, public service, and civic stewardship.</p></div>
        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/gv-blast.jpg" alt="Blast mitigation window film holding civic building glass fragments in place" title="Blast mitigation window film holding civic building glass fragments in place" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Safety</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Blast Mitigation Film That Meets Federal Standards</h3><p class="of-row__body">For courthouses, federal buildings, police facilities, and high-traffic public offices, glass hazard mitigation is part of the security conversation. 3M Scotchshield Ultra safety and security films are specified for blast and forced-entry applications where UFC 4-010-01, GSA glazing goals, and fragment retention need formal documentation.</p><p class="of-row__body"><a href="/benefits/blast-mitigation/">Blast mitigation window film</a> helps hold broken glass fragments together and in the frame, reducing flying-shard risk and creating additional time for evacuation, response, or lockdown procedures. For public entrances and lobby glass, our <a href="/benefits/safety-and-security/">safety and security window film</a> specifications pair the film with the right attachment system so the glass and frame work together.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/gv-energy.jpg" alt="Solar control film improving energy performance in a Philadelphia civic office" title="Solar control film improving energy performance in a Philadelphia civic office" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Energy</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Cut Energy Costs Across Your Entire Civic Facility</h3><p class="of-row__body">Many government buildings built before 1970 lack the thermal performance expected from modern glazing. Solar control film rejects up to 86% of heat gain before it enters the building, improving comfort for permit offices, administrative departments, public counters, and west-facing civic wings.</p><p class="of-row__body">The right <a href="/benefits/energy-savings/">energy-saving window film</a> can support LEED EA documentation, federal energy mandates, and capital-planning conversations without replacing historic or hard-to-source windows. Payback periods often fall in the 3-5 year range depending on glass area, orientation, and HVAC load.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/gv-uv.jpg" alt="Clear UV protection film preserving records and civic interior materials" title="Clear UV protection film preserving records and civic interior materials" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">UV Protection</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Protect the Documents and Assets Inside Philadelphia's Civic Buildings</h3><p class="of-row__body">Historic records, displayed awards, artwork, seal displays, wood finishes, and public furnishings all degrade under cumulative UV exposure. Clear UV film blocks 99% of ultraviolet radiation while preserving the daylight that makes many civic interiors feel open and accessible.</p><p class="of-row__body">Fading is irreversible, which is why conservation-minded facility teams treat UV control as prevention. We can pair <a href="/benefits/uv-blocking-window-film-philadelphia/">UV-blocking window film</a> data with resources from <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/buildings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ENERGY STAR for buildings</a> and product documentation for review.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/gv-privacy.jpg" alt="Privacy window film controlling sightlines in a public government office" title="Privacy window film controlling sightlines in a public government office" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Privacy</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Control Sightlines Without Closing Off Philadelphia's Public Buildings</h3><p class="of-row__body">One-way daytime privacy film helps permit offices, HR departments, administrative corridors, and sensitive processing areas control sightlines without covering windows or making public buildings feel closed off. Frosted and decorative films can also protect interior offices and jury areas.</p><p class="of-row__body">Privacy film can maintain daylight and openness while blocking external views, and solar/privacy combinations reduce glare on computer screens. That matters for screen-heavy public service work, where <a href="https://www.osha.gov/ergonomics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA ergonomics</a> guidance recognizes visual comfort as part of healthier workstations.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ FILM TYPES SLIDER ══ -->
    <section class="of-ftype" id="ofFilmTypes">
      <div class="of-ftype__track" id="ofFtypeTrack">
        <div class="of-ftype__slide of-ftype-active" data-slide="0"><img src="/images/gv-slider-blast.jpg" alt="Blast mitigation film for government building glass" title="Blast mitigation film for government building glass" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">01</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Blast Mitigation Film</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Clear safety film for federal, municipal, and public-safety glazing where fragment retention, blast mitigation, and documentation matter.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What blast mitigation standard does the film meet?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Scotchshield Ultra series meets UFC 4-010-01 GSA glazing standards used in federal buildings and military installations. Full compliance documentation is provided.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does blast film look different from regular glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>No. Scotchshield is optically clear with no tint and no reflection. The protective layers are invisible to the naked eye.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can blast mitigation film be installed in occupied government buildings?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Installation can be sequenced after hours or section by section so government operations continue with minimal disruption.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Request blast film specs →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="1"><img src="/images/gv-slider-security.jpg" alt="Security and anti-intrusion film for public safety building glass" title="Security and anti-intrusion film for public safety building glass" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">02</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Security &amp; Anti-Intrusion Film</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">A forced-entry delay layer for entry glass, public counters, police precincts, and administrative offices.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does security film stop forced entry?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Security film does not make glass unbreakable, but it holds broken glass together and slows entry through the opening, adding response time at public-facing glass.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What safety standard applies?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Security film can be documented against ANSI Z97.1 safety glazing performance and related manufacturer impact testing for the selected film and attachment system.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can it work with access control systems?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Film is a physical hardening layer that complements cameras, alarms, locks, and access control by delaying entry through glass.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Assess entry glass →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="2"><img src="/images/gv-slider-energy.jpg" alt="Solar control and energy window film for government buildings" title="Solar control and energy window film for government buildings" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">03</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Solar Control &amp; Energy Film</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">High-performance solar film for civic facilities managing heat gain, glare, sustainability reporting, and aging glazing.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How much heat can it reject?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Selected solar control films reject up to 86% of solar heat, depending on the film and glass type. We document SHGC, VLT, and TSER for review.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can it support LEED credits?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Solar control film can support LEED Energy and Atmosphere documentation through modeled performance improvements and manufacturer data.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What is the payback timeline for government buildings?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Payback depends on orientation, HVAC load, glass area, and film selection, with many commercial projects falling in the 3-5 year range. We provide performance data for your facility review.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Review energy film options →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="3"><img src="/images/gv-slider-uv.jpg" alt="UV protection film for civic records and government interiors" title="UV protection film for civic records and government interiors" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">04</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">UV Protection Film</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Clear UV-blocking film for records rooms, historic interiors, public displays, awards, and civic furnishings.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Will it protect records and displayed documents?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. UV film blocks 99% of ultraviolet radiation, the major driver of fading in paper, textiles, wood, and displayed civic materials.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does UV film darken historic windows?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Clear UV films are nearly invisible and are often selected when preserving the look of historic civic glass is a priority.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does this relate to conservation standards?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Conservation practice emphasizes reducing UV exposure because fading is irreversible. We provide product data that facility teams can review alongside AIC-style preservation goals.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Protect civic interiors →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="4"><img src="/images/gv-slider-privacy.jpg" alt="Privacy film for sensitive government office glass" title="Privacy film for sensitive government office glass" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">05</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Privacy Film</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">One-way, frosted, and decorative privacy films for HR offices, counters, interview rooms, and administrative corridors.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does one-way daytime privacy work?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>One-way privacy film relies on a light difference: the brighter side sees reflection, while the darker side sees through. It is useful for daytime public-facing glass.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What happens at night?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>When interior lights are brighter than outside, one-way privacy can reverse. For consistent privacy, frosted or opaque decorative film is the better government specification.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Is privacy film compatible with older glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Often yes, but we assess glass type, tint, coatings, and thermal stress risk before recommending a reflective, frosted, or clear privacy product.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Plan privacy zones →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="5"><img src="/images/gv-slider-decorative.jpg" alt="Decorative and wayfinding film for government office interiors" title="Decorative and wayfinding film for government office interiors" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">06</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Decorative &amp; Wayfinding Film</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Frosted bands, agency branding, privacy gradients, and durable graphics for public-service interiors.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can film brand government spaces?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Decorative film can carry seals, department names, directional graphics, or subtle civic patterns while remaining removable and glass-friendly.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Is frosted film useful for partitions?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Frosted film is ideal for interior partitions where staff need light transmission with visual privacy at counters, offices, and meeting rooms.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can custom graphics be updated later?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Professionally installed decorative film can be removed and replaced when departments move, signage changes, or public-service areas are reorganized.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Design civic glass graphics →</a></div></div>
      </div>
